' ── THE CHECKLIST CARD (owner chose I3, 2026-11-13; built stage 2) ─────────
' Form.Checklist draws a form as one running checklist: init's four pages in
' one card. The plan is the I3 implementation spec (§2 the card, §7 the
' mocks); the Executor's choices while building it are tagged S2-n. Init
' does not set the flag yet; stage 4 does. Every rule below applies only
' under the flag, so no golden of another form moved.
' ROW KINDS (Row.Kind; Executor S2-1, the spec named only Mark):
'   RowAnswer   options or a field; the only kind the cursor stops on
'   RowStatus   mark at 2, label at 4 (+Indent), Outcome at 18
'   RowCheck    mark at 2, "name — message" from 4, wrapping at 6; then
'               Evidence, Fix and Note at 6, in that order
'   RowDone     a finished page: worst mark, short name, outcome
'   RowAhead    a page not reached: ·, short name, "page n/4 · <asks>"
' A row with When{Row, Option} draws only while that row reads Option. A
' hidden row keeps its index; move, firstSelectable, lastSelectable, the
' ladder and the walls skip it; Selection and FieldValue still read it, so
' an answer survives closing and reopening. Indent 2 puts a sub-row's label
' at 6 (location, PATH, user.name, user.email).
' MARKS (marks.go, shared later by the stream printer):
'   done ✓/+ ok · needs ! warn · failed ×/x fail · later –/- idle ·
'   ahead ·/. idle · running ◐◓◑◒ or | / - \ brand, one per formTick ·
'   cursor ›/> brand. ASCII and Off draw the second set (plainGlyphs).
'   Tag gives the plain head: "ok: ", "warn: ", "error: ", or nothing.
'   Worst folds marks: × over ! over ✓ over –.
' COLUMNS: marks and › at 2; labels, facts, prose and check text at 4;
'   sub-row labels and check details at 6; outcomes and the grid at 18;
'   a field's ✓ or ! at 18 and its [ at 20; the chip ends at cw-3.
' FIELDS (Executor, S2-3): the focused field draws "_" after a typed value,
'   as §7's P2 mock does; an empty one shows its placeholder bare. Held
'   draws MarkDone's glyph, so ASCII reads "+".
' THE BORDER LABEL. Form.Border puts "╭── <label> ──…╮" on the top border,
'   the label in the title style, cut with … so two rules still close it.
' THE HEAD. The title row; the facts (header lines asFact accepts); the
'   prose (every other header line, at 4); one blank. No blank sits
'   between the title and its facts.
' SPACING. One blank after the head and after the done rows. One blank
'   before each stub, except a stub that already follows one of those.
'   One blank after a check with details when a check follows in the same
'   section. None after a stub, inside a section, or over the foot rule.
'   Under the card one blank, then the help at 2.
' THE LADDER (checklistLadder): one thing at a time, first fit wins:
'   1 spacing blanks, bottom-up   2 the blank under the card
'   3 the prose line              4 passed checks fold: "✓ n passed — a, b"
'   5 detail entries past each check's kept one (the fix, else the first
'     evidence line), bottom-up; a wrapped entry drops whole (S2-2: the
'     cut counts entries, so P1's evidence and two-row note are 2)
'   6 done rows fold: "<worst> pages 1-n   names"
'   7 the head's blank            8 ahead pages fold: "· pages n-4   names"
'   9 the body scrolls; ▲ ▼ ░ ride the right wall
' The help never drops. Its slot is the most lines any answer, field, run
' or blocker needs, at most 2; a longer help cuts its second line with ….
' THE HELP. An answer names its option as its cell does (Paint wins:
' "› keep as is — …"). A field reads "› <label> — <Reason>". A run reads
' "<label> — <Progress.Help>". A blocker reads Form.Blocked. Under ASCII
' the head's › draws >.
' AN OUTCOME TOO LONG for its row draws Row.Brief, the page's count form;
' a Brief still too long cuts its first item with … (fitOutcome). Only a
' done row cuts its first item (S2-18): an ahead or status row cuts its
' end, so "page n/4" stays at any width.
' A WORD TOO LONG for its line (a URL, a path) in check text or a detail
' line keeps its head and tail around … (clipMiddle; D8, S2-19). wrap
' never splits a word, so nothing else reaches the wall.
' THE KEY LINE (checklistKeys), at rest: "↑↓ row" while any row is a stop;
' "←→ choose" while the cursor's row has options; "type to edit" on a
' field; "pgup/pgdn scroll" (rank 4) only while the body scrolls; "enter
' <EnterWord>" ("run n steps", "next", "finish"; nil reads "next"); "esc
' back" past page 1, "esc quit" on it; "ctrl+c quit". No "on enter" list,
' no "N more below" (▼ says it), no topology. Blocked: "esc quit · ctrl+c
' quit", even over an open list; Enter does nothing. Progress: "running i
' of m — <label> · ctrl+c stop", or "stopping after <label> …". Under
' ASCII and Off the arrows read "up/down" and "left/right", as the dialogs
' over the card do (S2-22).
' TOO SMALL. A checklist that cannot hold 3 body rows past its last rung
' prints "terminal too small: WxH, need N rows", N counting the facts
' rows, over its key line without "↑↓ row" (S2-20).
' ESC ON PAGE 1 ASKS (D26): wizard-keys.puml.
' THE QUIT DIALOG DRAWS OVER THE DIMMED CARD: confirm-dialogs.puml.
' RENDER. Form.Render(w, h) is the form at rest as plain text, the cursor
' where it opens, trailing blanks cut: what cmd goldens will read.
' MEASURED (stage 2 goldens): every 80x24 mock in the I3 spec's §7 and its
' P1 120x36 mock match the drawn frames character for character.
' Pinned by internal/choiceui/checklist_test.go: TestAChecklistGoldenFrames
'   (testdata/checklist.txtar, 22 entries), TestAStatusRowIsNeverACursorStop,
